# Program Management Point of View

When forming a release-level or cross-team health assessment, the PM/TPM lens
requires reasoning about **patterns across features**, not just individual feature
status. A release can have zero RED features and still be in serious trouble.

## Portfolio-Level Risk Patterns to Always Check

### 1. Concentration risk

- If > 30% of committed features are in the same early phase (e.g. all still in
  Coding) with < 6 weeks to the CC gate → flag as `PHASE_CONCENTRATION_RISK`.
- Reason: if the whole portfolio slips at the same gate simultaneously, recovery
  is not possible by redistributing work.

### 2. Dark portfolio

- If > 20% of committed features have status updates ≥ 14 days old → the program
  has a **visibility problem**, not just individual feature problems.
- Lead the briefing with the visibility gap, not the individual dark features.

### 3. Gate compression

- Compute the window between consecutive binding gates (CCM → CG → PG).
- If any window is < 3 weeks → flag as `GATE_COMPRESSION`.
- Sub-3-week windows make QA, compliance, and stabilisation structurally impossible —
  the schedule itself is the risk, regardless of individual feature status.

### 4. Compliance clustering

- If > 3 features have unfiled security or legal (SDL/LEG) with < 6 weeks to CG →
  flag as `COMPLIANCE_CLUSTER`.
- One unfiled ticket = a feature risk. Multiple unfiled = a program process failure.
  The action is process-level (TPM to run a compliance checkpoint), not feature-level.

### 5. Owner absence

- If > 2 gate-lagging or dark features share the same TPM owner or assignee →
  name the **pattern**, not just each feature.
- "Three features owned by X are all dark" is a more actionable finding than
  three separate dark-feature bullets.

## RAG Verdict Adjustment for Portfolio Patterns

These patterns can **elevate** the release-level verdict beyond what individual
feature buckets suggest:

| Pattern | Minimum verdict |
|---|---|
| `PHASE_CONCENTRATION_RISK` (> 30% same early phase near gate) | YELLOW |
| `GATE_COMPRESSION` (any binding gap < 3 weeks) | YELLOW |
| `COMPLIANCE_CLUSTER` (> 3 features unfiled near CG) | YELLOW |
| `PHASE_CONCENTRATION_RISK` + `GATE_COMPRESSION` together | RED |
| `DARK_PORTFOLIO` (> 20% dark) AND gate < 4 weeks away | RED |

## Capacity Math at the Program Level

Before assigning a portfolio verdict, compute whether the **aggregate** open work
is completable in the remaining runway:

```
total_open_sp = sum of trulyOpenCount × avg_sp_per_ticket across all watching/gate-lagging features
available_capacity = working_days(today, CG_date) × assumed_team_sp_per_day
```

- If `total_open_sp > available_capacity` AND > 30% of features are contributors →
  surface as a program-level capacity gap, not just individual feature risks.
- State the numbers: "The portfolio carries ~N SP of open work; at current velocity
  there is only capacity for ~C SP before CG on `<date>`."

## Action Item Framing (PM lens)

PM-level action items address the **program**, not just the ticket:

| Wrong (feature-level) | Right (PM-level) |
|---|---|
| "Ask John to file SDL ticket for Feature X." | "Run a 30-min compliance checkpoint — 4 features are approaching CG with no SDL filed. One call resolves all four." |
| "Follow up on Feature A status update." | "All dark features are owned by Team Foo — escalate to their EM for a status sweep, not individual owners." |
| "Monitor Feature B for gate slip." | "Feature B's CC date is 12 days past the release CCM — ask RM today whether to extend or cut scope." |

## 7-Day Action List Rules (PM edition)

1. Address the **most systemic** risk first, not the most severe individual ticket.
2. Group actions by owner/team — one action item per team is more executable
   than five items each naming a different person.
3. Every action must have a named owner (person or role), a specific deliverable,
   and a deadline (not "ASAP" — use a day of the week or a date).
4. If gate compression is present, the first action is always: "Ask RM to confirm
   whether gate dates can flex — the current schedule leaves < 3 weeks between
   `<Gate A>` and `<Gate B>`."
5. Never use "monitor" as an action — replace with a specific ask that has a
   named owner and a concrete deliverable.

## Calibration by Release Phase

| Weeks to PG | Primary focus | Primary risk |
|---|---|---|
| > 8 weeks | CC date commits, child ticket creation | Uncommitted / un-scoped work |
| 4–8 weeks | Gate lagging, compliance filing, capacity math | Slipping features |
| 2–4 weeks | P0/P1 bugs, must-fix tickets, doc completion | Stabilisation failures |
| < 2 weeks | Must-fix closure only; new scope is off the table | Any new risk requires explicit RM decision |

Inside 2 weeks of PG: any feature not at PG Met or Shipped that raises a new
risk must be escalated to RM immediately — the program cannot absorb it through
normal channels.

## Staleness Is a Program Signal, Not Just a Feature Signal

- A single stale feature = team communication gap.
- Two or more stale features in the same team = EM accountability gap.
- More than 20% of the portfolio stale = program governance failure.

When dark features cluster by team, the action item targets the EM or program
lead, not the feature owner.

## Never Do

- Report "all features are on track" when portfolio-level patterns (concentration,
  compression, dark percentage) indicate systemic risk
- Assign seven separate action items for seven separate features when they share
  an owner, a team, or a root cause
- Use "monitor" or "keep an eye on" as a deliverable — these are not actions
- Omit the portfolio capacity calculation when > 4 features are in early phases
  near a gate
- Accept individual GREEN self-reports at face value without checking gate
  feasibility (per `release-gate-aware-reasoning.mdc`)

